1. What is an Interface in Java?

An interface is a blueprint of a class. It contains method declarations (and can also contain default and static methods since Java 8), but it cannot be instantiated.

An interface tells a class what to do, but the implementing class decides how to do it.


2. Why is WebDriver an interface?

Because Selenium needs a common API for different browser drivers. Each browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, etc.) implements the methods differently while exposing the same set of operations.


3. Why do we write WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();?
It supports runtime polymorphism.
Makes it easy to switch browsers.
Promotes loose coupling and maintainable code.
